  {
    "episode_id": 96064,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96064.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "Well, so first one, building a wall. Walls work. And that also sends a signal to everybody that we're trying to secure our country. And when President Biden said, I'm not going to build the wall, you know, you're leaving and you know, something I saw last week as I was going along the border. I mean, you're seeing acres and acres of, you know, 20 foot high stacks of wall materials. As well as you're seeing walls that were stopped, you know, right in the middle of a field. I mean, you know, here they're building a wall also and it stops. Like, well, where's the rest of the wall? Well, they stopped building it. What about that gate? Does that gate work? Well, it hasn't been hooked up to electric. Maybe it will. Maybe it won't. I mean, so you watched the physical barrier that is effective. And actually, let me give you an example. when I was with the riverine patrol that is paid for by the Texas taxpayers, the Department of Public Safety. And so those state troopers patrol a section of the river along in the Rio Grande, along the U.S. Mexican border, and they look for smugglers, human trafficking, drug trafficking. And there was a section of border wall that was three miles long, this wall that had been built. And it wasn't a public, it wasn't built by the federal government, it was built privately. And I asked, I said, look, this wasn't, this is new. And again, I know it was built a couple of years ago. And there was a cane sugar grower on the, you know, the farmer. And when he would go to burn his crops after to burn the cane to replant, drug smugglers would come and threaten his workers because the drug smugglers would hide drugs in his cane fields. And he would actually find, believe it or not, or so you'd actually find human bodies of human trafficking victims who had been told to hide in the cane and not come out. And they literally would be burned alive. uh... when he was burning his cane fields and the forest that i've had enough of this too many people died too many drugs to be here i'm tired of threats so he built a wall and guess what the drug smuggling in the human smuggling across his cane field basically ground to a halt It was successful. It was effective. And look, that's a small example of three-mile section. It's like it's an 1,800-mile border. But border walls work. And where they've been implemented, you've seen 90% to 95% reductions in the apprehensions along those sections of border, whether it's in California or Arizona or near El Paso, where they've actually put the walls in place.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
