    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96064.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "Again, through absolutely terrible decisions and hubris and pride in trying to, you know, has created, you know, has taken us from one of the most secure borders in American history to one of the least secure borders in American history. And there's no question that the damage to American society is very real. Something we haven't talked about yet, Armstrong, is drugs. It just came to light that more patrol has apprehended, as confiscated, more fentanyl this calendar year than they did the three previous calendar years combined. And what that is doing to our communities, let me just kind of step back and make this comment. We have an opioid crisis in our country. Ten years ago, we would lose 10 to 15,000 Americans to drug overdoses. Last year, 92,000 Americans died from drug overdoses, 92,000. That is more Americans that have died in all the wars that have been fought in my lifetime. I'm 49 years old. More Americans died for drug overdoses last year in one year alone than all the wars that have been fought in my lifetime. So that is a staggering statistic. And the amount of fentanyl coming across our southern border, because when it's on the border patrol, while it's down on the border, the border is like, look, they used to come right up this international bridge and come right to this processing station and I'd process them and it was kind of simple. But then the cartels realized, wait a minute, we can have them go over, have them go to the east by four miles and then I've got to pick them up, I've got to bust them over to the processing station. And while I'm doing that, they can run drugs over in the West. Right, so the human drug trafficking, the human trafficking operation is helping the drug trafficking operation operate at a whole new levels. And so, you know, in the Dallas area, one of the sheriffs there was saying that, look, a price for a hit of fentanyl, which was $50 in January when President Biden took his oath of office by June had dropped to $15. from $50 to $50. So the fentanyl is lowering the prices, increasing the supply, and more Americas are going to die in every community in this country. There is no part of our country that is going to escape the scourge of drugs that is coming across our southern border. It is impact, and I've met with families. that have lost loved ones to fentanyl, and it isn't necessarily a 20-year-old kid going to raise. Sometimes the drug overdoses, the majority age is actually 40 to 50, if you can believe that. That is actually where most of the drug deaths are, is 40 to 50 years old. And I've talked to a young woman who lost her grandmother to opioid overdoses. Right? So this is a very serious problem. It spans all generations. And the Biden administration is taking a terrible set of policy choices, which is exasperating one of the most serious problems that our country faces right now. And that is this opioid crisis. He has made this terribly worse. And I am very afraid for the numbers that we're going to see for 2021 for the number of deaths.",
